---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import FridgeAudit from "../components/FridgeAudit.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="The inside of a refrigerator, drawn flat in gold and teal on a warm near-black. Shelves run the full width of the frame, crowded with jars, bottles, cartons, egg boxes, wrapped parcels and a few sprigs of greens. The upper shelves are lit and full; the lower ones fall away into darkness where the contents can no longer be made out."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    There is a machine in your kitchen that has run without interruption since
    the day you moved in. You did not choose its shape. You have never
    questioned its necessity. You would struggle to describe how a household
    managed before it existed — and that struggle is not a gap in your
    education. It is the product.
  </p>

  <FridgeAudit />

  <h2>The argument</h2>
  <p>
    This course takes one position and holds it for twelve weeks. The
    refrigerator is not a neutral appliance. It is the control centre of
    micropolitics in the modern household, and an <strong
      >illusory fortress</strong
    > against temporal decay. By manufacturing expiration anxiety and hoarding illusions,
    it colludes with and sustains late capitalism's logic of food waste and overconsumption.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fortress works, which is the awkward part. Food really is colder and
    really does last longer. It simply does not matter, because the binding
    constraint was never the rate of decay — it was attention. The fridge does
    not prevent waste. It relocates it: out of the shop, where you would have
    had to decide against buying, and into your own bin three weeks later, where
    time has made the decision for you and nobody has to feel they chose it.
  </p>
  <p>
    You are not required to agree. You are required to argue, and the assessment
    rewards the student who presses the strongest objection — vaccine cold
    chains, reduced foodborne illness, year-round nutrition — over the one who
    agrees fluently.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    This course cannot be passed from the library. Almost every week asks you to
    bring evidence from a real kitchen, usually your own, and the seminar runs
    on what you found rather than on what you read.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Taste blind</strong> — fresh, three days refrigerated, and freeze-dried,
      without knowing which is which, and then argue about whether "freshness" is
      a physical state or a marketing achievement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Excavate a fridge</strong> — map a real interior as governed space,
      with its authority zones, its marginal shelves, and the unclaimed territory
      nobody will admit to owning.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run label forensics</strong> — bring ten confusing date labels, work
      out what the regulations actually require, and draft an honest replacement for
      each.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Audit your own waste</strong> — record a week of everything you throw
      out, then cost it in money and in calories. This is the number that changes
      people.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Design the dystopia</strong> — build the most exploitative smart fridge
      you can imagine, pitch it sincerely, then discover how much of it already exists.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Make something that needs no plug</strong> — a jar of quick ferment,
      or the operating rules for a community pantry.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You leave with four things: an ethnography of a real household, a critical
    essay, a counter-fridge proposal you have defended in front of the room, and
    a jar of pickles.
  </p>

  <h2>The shape of the semester</h2>
  <p>
    Four modules of three weeks. Each one takes the same claim and turns it
    another degree.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Disenchantment</strong> — the commodification of cold, from the ice
      trade to Freon, and the myth of modernity sold alongside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Micropolitics</strong> — the interior as a jurisdiction, and the door
      as a stage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The tyranny of time</strong> — date labels, systemic waste, and the
      stuffed fridge as emotional solace. Where the argument stops being interpretive
      and becomes measurable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resistance and reconstruction</strong> — surveillance capitalism in
      the kitchen, degrowth, fermentation, and what could be built instead.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    A postgraduate seminar, open across disciplines. It suits anthropology,
    design, environmental studies, science and technology studies, food studies,
    sociology and media. There is no technical prerequisite and no assumed
    background in food science — the theory is taught as we go.
  </p>
  <p>
    What it does assume is a willingness to look at your own kitchen and report
    honestly what is in it, including the parts that are embarrassing. The
    fieldwork is not optional and it cannot be borrowed.
  </p>
  <p>Rule yourself out if you want:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a food science or nutrition course — this is not one</li>
    <li>to learn to cook — you will make exactly one thing, badly</li>
    <li>
      to be told that refrigeration is simply good, or simply bad — the course
      argues neither and will make you defend whichever you arrive holding
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        The twelve tutorials, and what to bring to each. This is where the
        course actually happens.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>
        The weekly argument and its readings, including the week 7 deck on the
        tyranny of date labels.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>
        An ethnography, an essay, a counter-fridge, and participation. What each
        is worth and when it is due.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>
        Two convenors — one who reads objects, one who weighs what households
        throw away.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
